* 22 Oct 26 dm  New (S.75, TK-0.4).
* 23 Oct 26 dm  The wrapping loop is $tk_wrap now (the same algorithm, moved; the menu management page and the help box use it too).
* 24 Oct 26 dm  The lines of the explanation are wrapped like the sentence: the box cut a line at its edge, and the tail of the ai gate's 150-character sentence (tool.gates) never showed.
* 10 Nov 26 Windows port - level with Linux e3490a7f (the 28 Oct carry of ec8459b's sixth argument is in the 26 Oct line below).
* 26 Oct 26 dm  faults: an optional sixth argument, the title ("Run this command?" read over writes that are not commands); VAR.ARGS, so no existing call changes.
* 2 Nov 26 dm  S.88 P10: an optional seventh argument, the buttons (two or three; the last is Cancel and the default, the middle one of three answers 'alt'), for tool.gates's
*              leave question.  No existing call changes.
* 9 Nov 26 dm  S.186: before the box is drawn, the keys already typed are thrown away and it waits for a third of a second with no key, so that a held R (the
*              page's command and the box's Run) cannot open the box and answer it by key repeat.
* END-HISTORY
*
* START-DESCRIPTION:
*
*   call $tk_confirm(sentence, lines, typed, res, msg {, boxtitle {, btns}})
*
*     sentence  the command exactly as the tool will give it to $tk_run (the caller passes the same variable to both)
*     lines     the explanation under it, one to a field ($tk_explain makes it); may be null.  A line longer than the box is wrapped at spaces; an empty line stays empty
*     typed     null for a Run / Cancel box; a name the person must type for a second step (an account's name, for a destructive action or a gate that
*               reaches beyond SD)
*     res       returned: 'run' only when Run was chosen and, when a name was asked for, the name was typed exactly; 'cancel' for Cancel, Esc, an input
*               that ended, a session with no terminal; 'wrong' for a name that was not the one asked for
*     msg       returned: null for 'run'; else the line for the status bar (12826 nothing was run, or 12815 naming the name asked for)
*     boxtitle  OPTIONAL (the routine is VAR.ARGS, so every call with five arguments is as it was): the title of the box and of the box that asks for the name.  Left out or null it is
*               "Run this command?" (12817).  A page that is about to write or delete a record of its own, which is no command, gives a title that says so (12957 to 12960).
*     btns      OPTIONAL (2 Nov 26, S.88 P10): the buttons, a comma list of two or three labels in SCREEN MSGBOX's form.  Left out, null, or not two or three labels, they are
*               '&Run,&Cancel'.  THE LAST BUTTON IS CANCEL AND IS THE DEFAULT, whatever it is called.  The first is Run (res 'run', after the name when one is asked for); the middle one
*               of three returns res 'alt' and msg null, and asks for no name.  tool.gates's leave question is the one caller: Run the changes, Leave without them, Cancel.
*
*   THE DEFAULT BUTTON IS CANCEL: Enter alone never runs a command.  Run is chosen by its letter (r) or by moving to it.  The sentence is shown wrapped at
*   spaces, never cut, and never altered.  A session with no terminal cannot answer, so nothing runs ('cancel').  The typed name is compared as typed
*   (with surrounding spaces taken off), exactly.  This routine runs nothing and changes nothing: the caller runs the command with $tk_run when
*   res is 'run'.
*
* END-DESCRIPTION
*
* START-CODE

$internal
subroutine tk_confirm(sentence, lines, typed, res, msg, boxtitle, btns) var.args
$catalog $tk_confirm

$include sl.h
$include tk.h

   res = 'cancel'
   msg = sysmsg(12826)
   if system(25) then return

   call $sl_scr('get', scr)
   wrapw = scr<SL.S.W> - 16
   if wrapw > 100 then wrapw = 100
   if wrapw < 20 then wrapw = 20

   body = sysmsg(12816)
   call $tk_wrap(sentence, wrapw, pieces)
   for p = 1 to dcount(pieces, @fm)
      body<-1> = '  ' : pieces<p>
   next p
   if lines # '' then
      body<-1> = ''
*     24 Oct 26: the box CUTS a line at its edge (the tail of a 150-character sentence never showed), so each line of the explanation is wrapped like the sentence
      for li = 1 to dcount(lines, @fm)
         if lines<li> = '' then
            body<-1> = ''
         end else
            call $tk_wrap(lines<li>, wrapw, pieces)
            for p = 1 to dcount(pieces, @fm)
               body<-1> = pieces<p>
            next p
         end
      next li
   end

*  28 Oct 26: the title is the sixth argument, which a caller may leave out (VAR.ARGS): "Run this command?" over a box that is about a command, and the caller's own title over one that is
*  not (a record the page writes, a record it deletes).  An empty title is the default too.
   ttl = sysmsg(12817)
   if not(unassigned(boxtitle)) then
      if boxtitle # '' then ttl = boxtitle
   end

*  2 Nov 26: the buttons are the seventh argument, which a caller may leave out: Run and Cancel, or a list of its own whose LAST entry is Cancel and the default (tool.gates's leave
*  question: Run the changes, Leave without them, Cancel).  The middle one of three answers 'alt' and is not followed by the name.
   bl = '&Run,&Cancel'
   if not(unassigned(btns)) then
      if btns # '' then bl = btns
   end
   nb = dcount(bl, ',')
   if nb < 2 or nb > 3 then
      bl = '&Run,&Cancel'
      nb = 2
   end

*  9 Nov 26 (S.186, Fable's review): the letter that opens this box on a page (R on tool.gates, tool.jobs, tool.test, tool.catalog) is also Run's letter here, so a key held
*  down - the terminal repeats it about every 30 ms after half a second - would open the box and answer it.  The keys already typed are thrown away, and the box waits
*  until no key has come for a third of a second (at most 200 keys, so a key held for ever cannot keep it shut): a held key opens the box once, released; a second
*  press after that is read by the box as ever.
   dr.n = 0
   loop
      dr.c = keyin(0.35)
   while dr.c # '' and dr.n < 200 do
      dr.n += 1
   repeat
   screen msgbox body title ttl buttons bl default nb returning b else b = 0
   if nb = 3 then
      if b = 2 then
         res = 'alt'
         msg = ''
         return
      end
   end
   if b # 1 then return

   if typed # '' then
      fw = len(typed) + 4
      pr = sysmsg(12818, typed)
      tt = ttl
      screen ask pr width fw title tt returning ans then got = 1 else got = 0
      if not(got) then return
      if trim(ans) # typed then
         res = 'wrong'
         msg = sysmsg(12815, typed)
         return
      end
   end

   res = 'run'
   msg = ''
   return
end

* END-CODE
